::U2L2Q01::Cardiorespiratory endurance supports what? {=Sustained activity ~Only brief power ~Joint range only ~Reaction time}

::U2L2Q02::Which activity is primarily aerobic? {=A 30-minute brisk walk ~A 10-second sprint ~One explosive jump ~A brief maximal effort}

::U2L2Q03::Which activity is primarily anaerobic? {=A 10-second sprint ~Steady cycling ~A 20-minute walk ~Low-impact dancing}

::U2L2Q04::Exercise intensity means what? {=How hard the body is working ~How long activity lasts ~Which equipment is used ~How many weeks pass}

::U2L2Q05::Duration means what? {=How long activity lasts ~How hard the body works ~Heartbeats per minute ~Speech comfort}

::U2L2Q06::Heart rate is measured in what? {=Beats per minute ~Minutes per mile only ~RPE points ~Percent body composition}

::U2L2Q07::The 220 minus age calculation produces what? {=An estimated maximum heart rate ~An exact medical maximum ~A resting pulse ~A diagnosis}

::U2L2Q08::For fictional age 16, estimated maximum heart rate is what? {=204 bpm ~220 bpm ~196 bpm ~136 bpm}

::U2L2Q09::Using 60% of 204 gives approximately what? {=122 bpm ~102 bpm ~163 bpm ~204 bpm}

::U2L2Q10::Using 80% of 204 gives approximately what? {=163 bpm ~122 bpm ~204 bpm ~80 bpm}

::U2L2Q11::What is the instructional 60%â€“80% range for age 16? {=Approximately 122â€“163 bpm ~60â€“80 bpm ~163â€“204 bpm ~100â€“120 bpm}

::U2L2Q12::What does an RPE of 0 represent? {=Rest ~Moderate effort ~Vigorous effort ~Maximal required effort}

::U2L2Q13::Which RPE commonly represents moderate to somewhat hard effort? {=5â€“6 ~0 ~1â€“2 ~9â€“10}

::U2L2Q14::Speaking comfortably usually suggests what? {=Lower intensity ~Maximal intensity ~A medical diagnosis ~Anaerobic activity only}

::U2L2Q15::Speaking only short phrases usually suggests what? {=Higher intensity ~Rest ~Very light effort ~Exact target-zone success}

::U2L2Q16::Why combine heart rate, talk test, and RPE? {=No single measure is perfect ~A tracker is always exact ~More data guarantee safety ~RPE replaces all observations}

::U2L2Q17::Which is a normal non-diagnostic activity response? {=Elevated but controlled breathing ~A guaranteed medical condition ~Exact fitness level ~Proof that intensity is ideal}

::U2L2Q18::If assigned intensity is too high, what is appropriate? {=Slow down or reduce movement demand ~Increase pace ~Chase the target number ~Ignore warning signs}

::U2L2Q19::If assigned intensity is too low and conditions are safe, what may be appropriate? {=A modest increase in pace ~A maximal sprint ~Ignoring recovery ~Doubling every variable}

::U2L2Q20::Which rule overrides target heart-rate numbers? {=Safety and warning signs ~Device preference ~Competition ~Sweating}

::U2L2Q21::Age 15 gives which estimated maximum? {=205 bpm ~215 bpm ~195 bpm ~185 bpm}

::U2L2Q22::Age 17 gives which estimated maximum? {=203 bpm ~217 bpm ~193 bpm ~170 bpm}

::U2L2Q23::A steady walk with RPE 5 and comfortable brief sentences is most consistent with what? {=Moderate intensity ~Rest ~Maximal intensity ~Anaerobic only}

::U2L2Q24::Heart rate is in range, but the student becomes dizzy. What should happen? {=Stop and follow appropriate safety/help procedures ~Continue because the number is correct ~Increase pace ~Ignore dizziness}

::U2L2Q25::Why is sweating not an exact intensity measure? {=It is affected by many factors ~It always equals heart rate ~It proves aerobic activity ~It measures duration}

::U2L2Q26::A tracker is unavailable. Which methods remain valid? {=Talk test, RPE, observations, and manual pulse when appropriate ~No monitoring is possible ~Sweat only ~Guessing only}

::U2L2Q27::Which evidence suggests recovery? {=Breathing and perceived effort gradually move toward rest ~Effort rises after stopping ~Warning signs are ignored ~Heart rate must instantly equal rest}

::U2L2Q28::A stop-and-go court activity is often best classified as what? {=Mixed aerobic and anaerobic demand ~Only flexibility ~Only body composition ~Rest}

::U2L2Q29::Which interpretation is unsafe? {=Reach the target even when feeling unwell ~Use several methods ~Reduce excessive effort ~Treat the formula as an estimate}

::U2L2Q30::What is the correct reasoning sequence? {=Calculate, interpret, then adjust safely ~Calculate and obey the number blindly ~Exercise maximally, then estimate ~Use duration as intensity}

